## Goal
Run a principal component analysis of the gene count matrix and plot the samples on the first
two principal components, with each point coloured by its experimental condition, to show how
the six samples (A1-A3, B1-B3) group relative to one another.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| `counts_gene` (native) | `02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper` | `02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv` |
| `design` (native) | `01_prepare_samplesheets` | `01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv` |

## Method
All steps run in one Python script, `scripts/sample_pca.py`, in the `gars-bio` conda
environment (pandas 3.0.5, numpy 2.5.2, pydeseq2 0.5.4, scikit-learn 1.9.0, matplotlib 3.11.1),
submitted as a single batch job. Inputs are read only; nothing is written outside `results/`.

1. **Resolve inputs.** At run time, call
   `python3 _system/resolve_artifact.py --project projects/run-c3e50a58 --assay rnaseq_bulk --consumes counts_gene design`
   and read the two `resolved` paths from its JSON; stop with an error if `ok` is false.
2. **Load and align.** Read the count matrix (tab-separated); set `gene_id` as the index and
   drop the `gene_name` column. Read the design table (`sample_id,condition,group,replicate`).
   Stop with an error unless the count-matrix sample columns and the design `sample_id`s are
   the same set; reorder the design to the matrix's column order.
3. **Prepare counts.** The input is salmon's length-scaled estimates, which are not integers;
   round to the nearest integer (as DESeq2's `tximport`/`DESeqDataSetFromMatrix` route does).
   Drop genes whose count is zero in every sample.
4. **Variance-stabilise.** Build a pydeseq2 `DeseqDataSet` (samples x genes, metadata = design,
   `design="~condition"`) and apply `vst(use_design=False)` — a *blind* transform, so the
   condition labels play no part in the values the PCA sees (the DESeq2 `vst(blind=TRUE)`
   convention for sample-level QC).
5. **Select genes.** Keep the 500 genes with the highest variance across samples of the VST
   values (DESeq2 `plotPCA`'s default `ntop=500`); if fewer than 500 genes remain, use all of
   them. Record the number used.
6. **PCA.** `sklearn.decomposition.PCA` on the samples x genes matrix, centred (sklearn's
   default) and not scaled to unit variance; `n_components = min(n_samples, n_genes)` (6 here).
   Record each component's percentage of variance explained.
7. **Plot.** Scatter of PC1 (x) vs PC2 (y), one point per sample, coloured by `condition` with
   one fixed colour per level and a legend; each point labelled with its `sample_id`; axis
   labels carry the percentage of variance explained (e.g. `PC1 (xx.x%)`); equal axis aspect is
   not forced. Saved as PNG (300 dpi) and PDF.
8. **Write tables.** Per-sample PC scores (all components) joined with the design columns, and
   per-component variance explained.

